@!internal

--- @namespace coroutine
module nupp.runtime.standardcoroutine

--[[
Structured concurrency: a scope owns the tasks spawned into it.

Open a scope, spawn children into it, and the block that holds it does not end
until every one of them has run, been cancelled, or unwound.

```nupp
local greeting = ""

do
    local scope = coroutine.scope()
    const hello = scope:spawn(function(): string
        return "hello"
    end)
    const world = scope:spawn(function(): string
        return "world"
    end)

    greeting = hello:await() .. " " .. world:await()
end

assert(greeting == "hello world")
```

`scope` takes a `limit`, which parks `spawn` and `work` while that many children
are live, and a `timeoutms`, how long the scope may take. A running child
cooperates by calling `checkpoint`, which is where it learns the scope was
cancelled or its deadline passed.

```nupp
local total = 0

do
    local scope = coroutine.scope(timeoutms = 1000)
    const sum = scope:spawn("sum values", function(): integer
        local answer: integer = 0
        for value = 1, 1000 do
            coroutine.checkpoint()
            answer = answer + value
        end

        return answer
    end)

    total = sum:await()
end

assert(total == 500500)
```

## Growing task families

[](coroutine.suspension)'s combinators own a family that is complete when the call is
written: `all` cannot be handed a fourth body once it is running. `create` makes
a coroutine that inherits a handler and says nothing about its result, failure,
or lifetime. A server, a loading pipeline, or a scene needs somewhere to put
children found over time and one answer to what happens when the body returns, a
child fails, or the whole thing is cancelled.

CPU work on other threads is [](coroutine.workers), and a task scope is the only way
to it: `work` hands a child to a lane, and the scope owns the family of lanes
lazily and closes it through the suspension-aware path, so leaving the block
inside a frame parks rather than blocking the thread.

The scheduling contract is with the host rather than with each child. The host
sees one aggregate and decides when it runs; this decides which children run
then, in FIFO order, up to a bounded number of activations per host turn.
Nesting divides that bound rather than multiplying it, because the token belongs
to the turn.
]]

local source1 = require("nupp.tasks")
local clockContract = require("nupp.time.spi")
local selectedTime = require("nupp.runtime.timeprovider").provider

local function missingClock(...: any): never
    error("nupp.time: no clock provider is available", 2)
end

export type Cancellation = source1.Cancellation
export type Scope = source1.Scope
export type Status = source1.Status
export type Task<F> = source1.Task<F>
export const Cancellation = source1.Cancellation
export const Scope = source1.Scope
export const checkpoint = source1.checkpoint
export const deadline = source1.deadline
export const gather = source1.gather
export const iscancelled = source1.isCancelled
export const scope = source1.open
export const race = source1.race
export const run = source1.run
export const sleep: clockContract.Provider.["sleep"] = selectedTime and selectedTime.sleep or missingClock
export const sleepuntil: clockContract.Provider.["sleepuntil"] = selectedTime and selectedTime.sleepuntil
    or missingClock
export const wakeat: clockContract.Provider.["wakeat"] = selectedTime and selectedTime.wakeat or missingClock

--- @internal
export const value: table = do
    local original = rawget(_G, "coroutine") as any
    original.Cancellation = Cancellation
    original.Scope = Scope
    original.checkpoint = checkpoint
    original.deadline = deadline
    original.gather = gather
    original.iscancelled = iscancelled
    original.scope = scope
    original.race = race
    original.run = run
    original.sleep = sleep
    original.sleepuntil = sleepuntil
    original.wakeat = wakeat
    yield original
end
